A||N||7||kgrimm||Clutch Troubles||||1||
Z||000000||kgrimm||09-27-2000||08:15 AM||||I'm having clutch trouble with my 85 V6 4sp.  I've search the archives and read the infamous clutch thread, but thought I should post my problems for additional advice.  The clutch pedal was slow to return from the floor, but otherwise the clutch seemed to work ok.  After sitting for a couple of monthes, the clutch pedal wouldn't depress.  The clutch was engaged as I could not get it into gear as it would grind.  Rebuilt master cylinder and replaced slave cylinder.  Pedal still would not move.  A few monthes later.  The clutch pedal will move and you can put it into gear, but when you relase the pedal, the clutch doesn't engage.  Any thoughts or suggestions would be greatly appreciated.  Thanks in advance.||||reg||1
Z||000001||StuGood||09-27-2000||08:39 AM||||I wonder if things won't move because of problems inside the bellhousing - such as: the pressure plate diaphragm spring lost its temper  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"> (pun intended)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">, and now the throwout bearing is jammed into the partially-bent fingers (of the pressure plate, not <B>your</B> fingers?<P>Just speculation... ||||reg||5
Z||000002||Carrolles||09-27-2000||09:03 AM||||Does the clutch slip or just not engage at all?  It sounds like the throwout bearing is binding on the input shaft.||||reg||1
Z||000003||JohnnyK||09-27-2000||05:47 PM||||heheh.. Your in my boat.. Get droppin' the cradle!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||kgrimm||09-28-2000||07:48 PM||||I plan on dropping the cradle next weekend.  The consensus seems to be that it is something internal, but I'm considering on replacing the clutch arm and clutch pedal with the cast and steel units respectively.  I'm not sure whether it will help in the end, but I figure that it is probably better to cover all the bases.  I have a lot of exhaust manifold bolts broken also.  I plan on tackling that along with valve stem seals.  Can I get the valve covers off without removing the plenums?  I'm afraid of what I might get into.  I guess only time will tell.||||reg||1
Z||000005||StuGood||09-28-2000||08:19 PM||||The manuals I read acted like you could get valve covers off w/o removing plenum (upper plenum + intermediate manifold), but I never figured out how.<P>It's not a big deal getting them off, anyway.  Thread the bolts back into their repective holes, so they didn't get lost.  Be careful with the plenum, though, they are probably <B>not</B> cheap!    ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Carrolles||09-28-2000||08:42 PM||||It's no big deal removing the plenum.  With it off you could do some clean-up on it.  You could gasket port match the upper to the middle and the bottom side of the middle.  This would also be a good time to look at all of the vacuum tubing the live between the upper and middle plenums.<P>I used oven cleaner to clean out all the baked on gunk at the inlet for the EGR in the upper plenum.||||reg||1
Z||000007||kgrimm||10-17-2000||12:34 PM||||The problem was the clutch fork shaft was frozen in its bores.  Sliding the shaft upward allowed me to clean up the shaft and now it works smoothly.  Problem solved.  The valve covers can be removed without removing the intake.  A swivel and long 1/4" extension help.||||reg||1
